tel 1.0

base chiaroscuro
import soundness

info   structural comparison of two values: decomposition into fields and elements, and a juxtaposition of the differences
detail
    _Chiaroscuro_ is the contrast behind a failed assertion. Where a test framework would
    print "expected X but found Y" as two `toString`s and leave the reader to diff them by
    eye, it **decomposes** both values — case classes into fields, collections into aligned
    elements, optionals into presence or absence, text into characters — and **juxtaposes**
    the two decompositions, so the report is a tree with the differing leaves marked: an age
    which differs by two, a list with one element substituted, a text with the changed
    characters banded.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    Person(t"Jo", 47).decompose
    Person(t"Jo", 47).contrast(Person(t"Jo", 49))
    // Juxtaposition.Collation(t"Person", List(t"name" -> Same(t"Jo"), t"age" -> Different(t"47", t"49", t"+2")), t"Person(Jo,47)", t"Person(Jo,49)")
    ```

    Three typeclasses do the work. `Decomposable` turns a value into a `Decomposition` — a
    `Primitive` leaf, a `Product` of named fields, a `Sequence` of elements or a `Sum`
    wrapping its variant — and is derived for any case class, enum, collection or optional,
    with every remaining leaf rendered through `Inspectable`, so a failure shows exactly what
    `.inspect` shows. `Contrastable` turns two values into a `Juxtaposition` — `Same`,
    `Different` (with an optional difference such as `+2`) or a `Collation` of labelled
    children — and is derived likewise, with hand-written foundations for numbers (which
    report the signed difference), sets (which report what each side has alone), exceptions
    and text. Sequences are aligned through dissonance's diff, so an insertion shows as an
    insertion rather than as every later element differing, and a changed element as one
    substitution broken down to the field which changed. `Similarity` is the pluggable notion
    of "similar enough to pair up" for that refinement.

    The probably test framework calls `contrast` on the two sides of a failed `_ == expected`
    assertion, records the `Juxtaposition` as a `Verdict.Detail.Compare`, and flattens it to
    `TestEvent.CompareRow`s for a host; `Harness#capture` records a `decompose`d value.

    The library has two components. `chiaroscuro.core` (JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native) is
    the typeclasses and the two trees; `chiaroscuro.render` adds `JuxtapositionPalette` and
    the `teletypeables.juxtapositionTeletype` given, which renders a `Juxtaposition` as a
    styled table of differences for a terminal. Everything is exported to the `soundness`
    umbrella; the `teletypeables` choice package merges with the umbrella's own, and its
    given is imported by name, since `import soundness.*` does not import givens — and
    escapade renders any `Showable` value unstyled, so a call site which forgets the import
    prints the enum's text rather than failing to compile.

refer chiaroscuro.Contrastable
refer chiaroscuro.Decomposable
refer chiaroscuro.Juxtaposition
refer chiaroscuro.Decomposition
refer probably.Test

entry .decompose   `value.decompose`: the value as a `Decomposition`, through its `Decomposable`
    An extension on any value with a `Decomposable`, which is derived for nearly everything:
    `24.decompose` is `Decomposition.Primitive(t"Int", t"24", 24)`, and a case class is a
    `Decomposition.Product` of its fields.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val x: Optional[Int] = 12
    x.decompose   // Decomposition.Sum(t"Optional", Decomposition.Primitive(t"Int", t"12", 12), 12)
    List('a', 'b').decompose
    ```

entry .contrast   `left.contrast(right)`: the two values juxtaposed, through their `Contrastable`
    An extension on any value with a `Contrastable`, derived for case classes, enums,
    collections and anything `Decomposable` or `Showable`. The left value is the expected
    one, the right the observed one, which is how a failed assertion's rendering labels
    them. Equal values give `Juxtaposition.Same`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    24.contrast(24)
    t"alpha".contrast(t"alphb")
    List(1, 2, 3).contrast(List(1, 3))
    ```

entry .Contrastable   how two values of a type are juxtaposed into a `Juxtaposition` of their differences
  detail
      The typeclass behind `contrast`. Its `.derived` given resolves in this order: a
      `Contrastable.Foundation` for the type (the hand-written instances for numbers, sets,
      exceptions, text and `Decomposition` itself); a case-class derivation, which
      juxtaposes field by field into a `Collation` labelled by field name; an enum
      derivation, which juxtaposes the two variants' decompositions; and otherwise the
      juxtaposition of the two values' `Decomposition`s, which is what collections,
      optionals and nested structures use. A type with only a `Showable` falls to
      `Foundation.showable`: `Same` when equal, `Different` of the two shown texts otherwise.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)
      case class Organization(name: Text, ceo: Person, staff: List[Person])

      val expected = Organization(t"Acme", Person(t"John", 49), List(Person(t"Janet", 19), Person(t"Paweł", 32)))
      val observed = Organization(t"Acme", Person(t"John", 43), List(Person(t"Paul", 32), Person(t"Janet", 19)))
      expected.contrast(observed)
      ```

      A `Contrastable` is a pure typeclass; `#contramap` makes one for another type from a
      function into this one.
  refer chiaroscuro.contrast
  refer chiaroscuro.Juxtaposition
  refer chiaroscuro.Decomposable
  entry .derived   the inline given which derives a `Contrastable` for any type, in implicit scope
      Foundation first, then product, then sum, then by decomposition.
  entry .Derivation   the wisteria derivation of `Contrastable` for products and sums
    detail
        A `Derivable[Contrastable]`: `conjunction` juxtaposes a case class field by field
        into a `Collation` (or `Same` of the shown value when the two are equal), and
        `disjunction` an enum by its variants' decompositions. Reached through `.derived`
        rather than called directly.
    entry .conjunction   a case class, field by field
    entry .disjunction   an enum, by its variants
  entry .Foundation   the hand-written instances, and the trait an instance of the caller's own extends
    detail
        `Contrastable.Foundation` as a **trait** is a `Contrastable` with the one abstract
        method `#juxtaposition`; `.derived` tries a `Foundation` before any derivation, so a
        type whose contrast should not be structural (a type with an identity, or a number
        with a meaningful difference) provides one in its companion. As an **object** it
        holds the standard foundations, each in implicit scope, and inherits the
        lowest-priority `showable` from `Contrastable.Protofoundation`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        3.contrast(5)        // Juxtaposition.Different(t"3", t"5", t"+2")
        1.5.contrast(1.25)
        Set(1, 2, 3).contrast(Set(2, 3, 4))
        ```
    entry .decomposition   two `Decomposition`s, juxtaposed structurally
    entry .int   two `Int`s: `Same`, or `Different` with the signed difference
    entry .short   two `Short`s, as `.int`
    entry .byte   two `Byte`s, as `.int`
    entry .long   two `Long`s: `Same`, or `Different` with the signed difference, `+2` or `-3`
    entry .float   two `Float`s, as `.double`
    entry .double   two `Double`s: `Different` with the signed difference to four significant figures
        Both sides are shown to enough decimal places to tell them apart; the difference is
        shown with a plus sign when positive.
    entry .set   two `Set`s of a `Showable` element: what each side has alone
        The difference reads `+{4}` for elements only on the right, `-{1}` for those only on
        the left, or `-{1}╱+{4}` for both, with sets of more than five elements elided.
    entry .exception   two `Exception`s: the same when their classes and messages are
    entry .string   two `String`s, as `.text`
    entry .text   two `Text`s, character by character
        The characters are aligned with dissonance's diff, so an insertion or deletion is
        shown as one rather than as every later character differing; the result is a
        `Collation` named `Text` whose children are single characters, which the terminal
        renderer shows as two banded lines.
    entry #juxtaposition   the juxtaposition of two values
  entry .Protofoundation   the base of `Foundation`'s companion, holding the lowest-priority `showable` given
    detail
        `Contrastable.Foundation` inherits `showable` from here, so every foundation outranks it.
    entry #showable   any `Showable` type: `Same` when equal, `Different` of the two shown texts
  entry .nothing   a `Contrastable` which reports every pair as `Same`, showing the left value's decomposition
      Inline; needs a `Decomposable` for the type. For a position which requires a
      `Contrastable` but has nothing to compare.
  entry .juxtaposition   juxtaposes two `Decomposition`s of any shape, with a type name for the result
      Equal references are `Same`; two `Primitive`s are `Different` by their texts; two
      `Sequence`s are aligned by `.comparison`; two `Product`s are a `Collation` over the
      union of their field names, a field absent on one side compared against a blank; two
      `Sum`s compare their variants (naming both types, `Circle/Square`, when they differ);
      and two decompositions of different shapes are `Different` by kind,
      `<product>`/`<sequence>`.
  entry .comparison   aligns two arrays of decompositions with a diff, as a `Collation` of labelled rows
      Each row is labelled by its indices: `3` for an element at the same index on both
      sides, `³╱₂` (superscript left, subscript right) when the indices differ, ` ╱₂` for an
      insertion, `³╱ ` for a deletion. A substituted element is juxtaposed recursively, so a
      record with one changed field shows as that field's difference. The diff is
      dissonance's `rdiff` with a window of ten.
  entry #juxtaposition   the juxtaposition of two values of the type
  entry #contramap   a `Contrastable` for another type, by mapping it to this one

entry .Decomposable   how a value is taken apart into a `Decomposition` for display
  detail
      The typeclass behind `decompose`, and the one `Harness#capture` and the assertion
      macros use to render both sides of a failure. Its `derived` given resolves in order: a
      `Decomposable.Base` for the type (`Text`, `Int`, `String`, `Decomposition`); a
      case-class derivation into a `Product` of named fields; an enum derivation into a
      `Sum`; `Any`, `AnyRef` and `Matchable` as an `Any` leaf; an `Optional` as a `Sum`
      named `Optional` of the value or an `Unset` leaf shown as `∅`; and every remaining
      leaf through `Inspectable`, whose own derivation always succeeds, so a `Char` renders
      `'a'`, a `Double` is not rounded by a `Decimalizer` in scope, and a type with neither
      falls to `Inspectable`'s marked `toString`. The companion's `.list`, `.sequence` and
      `.frozenArray` decompose collections element by element. The companion inherits
      `derived`, `primitive`, `any` and `Derivation` from `Decomposable2`, and `fallback` from
      `Decomposable3`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

      Person(t"Bill", 29).decompose
      // Decomposition.Product(t"Person", Map(t"name" -> Primitive(t"Text", t"Bill", t"Bill"), t"age" -> Primitive(t"Int", t"29", 29)), Person(t"Bill", 29))

      val x: Optional[Int] = Unset
      x.decompose   // Decomposition.Sum(t"Optional", Decomposition.Primitive(t"Unset", t"∅", Unset), Unset)
      ```

      Decomposition renders leaves through `Inspectable`, not `Showable`: a test failure is
      read by a programmer, so it should show what `.inspect` shows, and a type whose
      `Showable` and `Inspectable` disagree decomposes as the latter.
  refer chiaroscuro.decompose
  refer chiaroscuro.Decomposition
  refer spectacular.Inspectable
  entry .Base   the hand-written leaf instances, and the trait an instance of the caller's own extends
    detail
        `Decomposable.Base` as a **trait** is a `Decomposable` which `derived` tries before
        any derivation; as an **object** it holds the standard leaves.
    entry .text   `Text` as a `Primitive` named `Text`
    entry .int   `Int` as a `Primitive` named `Int`
    entry .string   `String` as a `Primitive` named `String`
    entry .decomposition   a `Decomposition` as itself
    entry #decomposition   the decomposition of a value
  entry .list   any `List` of a decomposable element, as a `Sequence` named `List`
  entry .sequence   any `Sequence` of a decomposable element, as a `Sequence` named `Sequence`
  entry .frozenArray   a frozen `Array[element]^{}` of a decomposable element, as a `Sequence` named `Array`
  entry #decomposition   the decomposition of a value

entry .Decomposable2   the base trait of `Decomposable`'s companion which holds `derived`, `primitive`, `any` and `Derivation`
  hidden
  detail
      A priority layer: its members are reached as `Decomposable.derived`,
      `Decomposable.primitive`, `Decomposable.any` and `Decomposable.Derivation`, which the
      companion inherits from here.
  entry #derived   the inline given which derives a `Decomposable` for any type, in implicit scope
      Reached as `Decomposable.derived`. Base, then product, then sum, then `Any`, then
      `Optional`, then `Inspectable`.
  entry #primitive   a `Decomposable` which renders every value as a `Primitive` of the given type name, by `toString`
  entry #any   a `Decomposable` which renders every value as a `Primitive` named `Any`, by `toString`
      What the assertion macros use when no better instance is derivable for a test's result
      type.
  entry #Derivation   the wisteria derivation of `Decomposable` for products and sums
    entry .conjunction   a case class as a `Product` of its fields, by label
    entry .disjunction   an enum as a `Sum` of its variant's decomposition

entry .Decomposable3   the lowest priority layer of `Decomposable`'s companion, holding `fallback`
  hidden
  detail
      Reached as `Decomposable.fallback`, which the companion inherits from here.
  entry #fallback   the lowest-priority given: a `Primitive` named `Any` by `toString`, for any type

entry .Decomposition   a value taken apart for display: a leaf, a product of fields, a sequence or a sum
  detail
      The four cases: `Primitive(typeName, value, ref)` is a leaf with its rendered text;
      `Product(name, values, ref)` a case class with its fields by label; `Sequence(name,
      values, ref)` a collection's elements; and `Sum(name, value, ref)` an enum or
      `Optional` wrapping its variant. Each keeps the original value as `#ref`, which
      `Contrastable.juxtaposition` compares first, so equal references are `Same` without a
      walk. `#text` renders the tree over several lines, indented; `#short` is a one-line
      summary (`[..3..]` for a sequence, the type name for a product); and `.inspectable`
      makes `inspect` the same as `#text`.
  refer chiaroscuro.Decomposable
  refer chiaroscuro.Juxtaposition
  entry .apply   a `Decomposition` from an optional one, with an `Unset` leaf for absence
  entry .inspectable   renders a decomposition as its `#text`
      Hand-written rather than derived: the derived instance's anonymous class is rejected
      by the Scala.js pipeline.
  entry #ref   the original value
  entry #text   the tree rendered over several lines, each level indented by two spaces
      A product's fields appear as `name:` with their values beneath; a sequence's elements
      are numbered from zero; a sum shows as `Name.` before its variant.
  entry #text2   a one-line rendering: `Name(field: value, …)`, `[a, b]`, `Name:value`
  entry #short   a one-line summary: a leaf's text, `[..n..]` for a sequence, the name of a product
  entry #multiline   appends the multi-line rendering to the `TextBuilder` in scope, at an indent

entry .Juxtaposition   the result of comparing two values: the same, different, or a collation of labelled differences
  detail
      `Same(value)` is one text for both sides; `Different(left, right, difference)` has the
      expected text, the observed text and an optional summary of the difference (`+2`,
      `-{1}╱+{4}`); `Collation(typeName, comparison, left, right)` is a labelled list of
      child juxtapositions — field names for a product, index labels for a sequence, one
      per character for text — with the two sides' whole texts. A collation whose children
      are all single characters is a text comparison, which the terminal renderer shows as
      two banded lines rather than as a table.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      t"alpha".contrast(t"alphb") match
        case Juxtaposition.Collation(name, comparison, left, right) => name
        case Juxtaposition.Different(left, right, _)                 => t"different"
        case Juxtaposition.Same(_)                                   => t"same"
      ```

      The probably framework flattens a juxtaposition to `TestEvent.CompareRow`s, pre-order
      with depth, for a host; `teletypeables.juxtapositionTeletype` renders one for a
      terminal.
  refer chiaroscuro.contrast
  refer chiaroscuro.Contrastable
  refer chiaroscuro.JuxtapositionPalette
  entry #singleChar   whether both sides are at most one character: the shape of a text comparison's children
  entry #leftWidth   the expected side's width in characters, summed over a collation's children
  entry #rightWidth   the observed side's width in characters, summed over a collation's children

entry .Similarity   whether two values are similar enough to be shown as one substitution rather than a deletion and an insertion
  detail
      Comparison alone can only say *same* or *different*, so a raw diff of two sequences
      reports a changed element as a deletion beside an unrelated insertion. `Similarity`
      is the typeclass for the refinement which pairs a removed element with an added one —
      a record with the same identifier and a changed field — so that the two are shown as
      one substitution broken down to the field which changed. It is contravariant, so an
      instance for a supertype serves its subtypes; `.generic` is equality.
  refer chiaroscuro.Contrastable
  entry .generic   the given for any type: similar when equal
  entry #similar   whether two values are similar

entry .JuxtapositionPalette   the colours a rendered juxtaposition uses, in sRGB
  detail
      A `Palette` (iridescence) with the roles a difference table needs: `#unaccented` for
      rules and margins, `#informative` for unchanged values, `#subdued` for de-emphasis,
      `#positive` for the expected side and `#negative` for the observed side, besides the
      inherited `foreground` and `background`. A real trait rather than a structural
      refinement, since structural selection needs runtime reflection, which Scala Native
      lacks. The `teletypeables.juxtapositionTeletype` given takes one as a contextual
      parameter; the probably framework no longer renders, so a host supplies its own.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given palette: JuxtapositionPalette = new JuxtapositionPalette:
        def background: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.Black
        def foreground: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.White
        def unaccented: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.DimGray
        def informative: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Silver
        def subdued: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
        def positive: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.LimeGreen
        def negative: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Red
      ```
  refer chiaroscuro.teletypeables
  entry #Form   the colour form: `Srgb`
  entry #unaccented   rules, margins and the border of a table
  entry #informative   values which are the same on both sides
  entry #subdued   de-emphasised text
  entry #positive   the expected side
  entry #negative   the observed side

entry .teletypeables   the terminal rendering of a `Juxtaposition`, a given to import by name
  detail
      In `chiaroscuro.render`. The given is not in `Juxtaposition`'s implicit scope, and
      escapade renders any `Showable` value unstyled, so a call site which fails to import
      it degrades silently — printing `Collation(Text,List(…))` — rather than failing to
      compile.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import teletypeables.juxtapositionTeletype
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      given charMeasurable: Char is Measurable = _.toString.tt.metrics

      given palette: JuxtapositionPalette = new JuxtapositionPalette:
        def background: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.Black
        def foreground: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.White
        def unaccented: Color in Srgb  = WebColors.DimGray
        def informative: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Silver
        def subdued: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
        def positive: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.LimeGreen
        def negative: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Red

      24.contrast(24).teletype.plain                // t"The value 24 was expected"
      t"alpha".contrast(t"alphb").teletype.plain
      ```
  entry .juxtapositionTeletype   renders a `Juxtaposition` as styled terminal text
      Needs a `Text is Measurable` (for column widths) and a `JuxtapositionPalette`. A
      `Same` renders as _The value `v` was expected_; a `Different` as _The result `right`
      did not equal `left`_; a `Collation` of single characters as two banded lines, the
      expected above the observed with differing characters highlighted and character
      offsets in the margins, wrapped at 110 columns; and any other `Collation` as a table
      with the tree of labels, an `Expected` column, an `Observed` column and a `Details`
      column holding each difference (or the Levenshtein distance of two differing texts).
